course: dictionary
lesson: 12293
title: Convincingly: believing and winning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Convincingly is an adverb. It tells you how something is done. It means in a way that makes people believe something is true or real. «The actor played the villain convincingly, and the children hid behind their chairs.» The clue is that the children believed him.
explain: The word can also mean clearly, with a big gap between the winner and the loser. «The home team won convincingly, 6 goals to 1.» Nobody needs to argue about who was better. Ask yourself: is someone being believed, or is the result clear and big?
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Priya argued convincingly that the school needed a garden» still makes sense with "in a way that makes people believe it", so you have the right meaning. If the swapped meaning sounds silly, try the other one.
fact: Convincingly is an adverb, and it comes from the adjective convincing.
fact: «Convincingly» means in a way that makes people believe something is true or real.
fact: «Convincingly» can also mean clearly and by a big margin, as in «won convincingly».
fact: The other forms are more convincingly and most convincingly.
fact: Actors play parts convincingly, and pupils argue convincingly when they give strong reasons.
exercise: «The actor played the villain so convincingly that the children hid behind their chairs.» What does convincingly mean here? | In a late and slow way | Without any costume | In a quiet and gentle way | *In a way that makes people believe it is real
exercise: «The home team won convincingly, 6 goals to 1.» What does convincingly mean here? | Only just, by one goal | *Clearly, by a big margin | Very quietly | Angrily and unfairly
exercise: Find and copy the adverb in this sentence: «Priya argued convincingly that the school needed a garden.» | Priya | *convincingly | argued | school
test: «Tom explained his plan so convincingly that his teacher said yes.» Convincingly here means | Loudly, so everyone could hear | *In a way that made his teacher believe the plan | Sadly and slowly | Badly and quickly
test: Which sentence uses convincingly to mean by a big margin? | «The rain fell gently.» | *«The swimmers won convincingly.» | «The bell rang loudly.» | «The pupils sang quietly.»
exam: Which is the superlative form of convincingly? | more convincingly | very convincingly | convincinglest | *most convincingly
